---
import ResourceLayout from '../../components/ResourceLayout.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import InfographicViewer from '../../components/InfographicViewer.astro';
import ExternalResource from '../../components/ExternalResource.astro';
---

<ResourceLayout
  icon="👶"
  eyebrow="Pregnancy, children, and prevention"
  title="Family Health"
  introduction="Learn about pregnancy care, children's healthcare, vaccinations, routine checkups, and preventive health services available in British Columbia."
>
  <VideoEmbed
    title="Children's Health for Newcomers"
    videoId="IRU82cJsDcc"
    description="Watch this short video to learn about children's healthcare, vaccinations, routine checkups, and how families can access healthcare services in British Columbia."
  />

  <InfographicViewer
    title="Children's Health: A Guide for Newcomers"
    description="Learn about routine childhood healthcare, vaccinations, developmental checkups, and when to seek medical care."
    previewSrc="/infographics/mental-health/childrens-health-en.png"
    previewAlt="Preview of the Children's Health infographic"
    languages={[
      {
        label: 'English',
        href: '/infographics/mental-health/childrens-health-en.png',
        lang: 'en',
      },
      {
        label: 'ਪੰਜਾਬੀ',
        href: '/infographics/mental-health/childrens-health-pa.png',
        lang: 'pa',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ExternalResource
    icon="🇨🇦"
    source="HealthLink BC"
    title="Children's Health"
    description="Find trusted information about childhood illnesses, immunizations, development, nutrition, and healthy parenting."
    href="https://www.healthlinkbc.ca/healthlinkbc-files"
  />

  <section>
    <h2>Pregnancy care</h2>
    <p>
      Pregnancy care may be provided by a family doctor, midwife, obstetrician,
      or nurse practitioner. Regular appointments help monitor the health of the
      pregnant person and the developing baby throughout pregnancy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Healthcare for babies and children</h2>
    <p>
      Babies and children should have regular checkups to monitor growth,
      development, nutrition, hearing, vision, and overall health. These visits
      are also an opportunity to discuss feeding, sleep, behaviour, and other
      concerns with a healthcare provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Vaccinations</h2>
    <p>
      Routine immunizations protect children and adults from serious infectious
      diseases. Your healthcare provider or local public health unit can explain
      which vaccines are recommended based on your child's age and vaccination
      history.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>School and community health services</h2>
    <p>
      Many schools and local public health programs offer services such as
      immunization clinics, hearing and vision screening, dental programs, and
      other preventive health services for children and families.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Preventive care</h2>
    <p>
      Preventive healthcare includes routine checkups, developmental screening,
      prenatal care, blood pressure monitoring, and age-appropriate screening
      tests. Regular visits help identify health concerns early.
    </p>
  </section>

  <aside class="notice">
    <strong>Know when to seek emergency care.</strong>
    <p>
      Call 911 or go to the nearest emergency department if a child has severe
      difficulty breathing, is difficult to wake, has a seizure, develops blue
      lips, has major bleeding, or appears seriously unwell.
    </p>
  </aside>
</ResourceLayout>